(CODE-DOC) Tj T*
(Campus Reachability and Emergency Route Audit) Tj T*
(Using Recursive DFS and BFS in Python) Tj T*
(Submitted By) Tj T*
(Name: Kanuri Thanush) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Roll Number: 25MVCAIR0059) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Course: MCA) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Language Used: Python 3) Tj T*
(Problem Statement) Tj T*
(The objective of this project is to develop a graph-based campus auditing system that) Tj T*
(analyzes the connectivity of different areas within a campus. The campus consists of several) Tj T*
(locations represented as vertices, while the walkways connecting these locations are) Tj T*
(represented as undirected edges.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The application performs three major operations:) Tj T*
(1. Identify all connected components using Recursive Depth First Search \(DFS\).) Tj T*
(2. Perform DFS and BFS traversals from a given emergency station \(source vertex\).) Tj T*
(3. Find the shortest route from the emergency station to requested destination vertices) Tj T*
(using the Breadth First Search parent array.) Tj T*
(The program must always process neighbouring vertices in increasing numerical order so that) Tj T*
(every execution produces the same output regardless of the order in which edges are entered.) Tj T*
(Objective) Tj T*
(The main objectives of this project are:) Tj T*
(- To represent a campus as an undirected graph.) Tj T*
(- To identify every connected component of the graph.) Tj T*
(- To assign every vertex the smallest numbered vertex of its connected component.) Tj T*
(- To perform Recursive DFS traversal.) Tj T*
(- To perform Queue-based BFS traversal.) Tj T*
(- To compute minimum-edge routes using BFS.) Tj T*
(- To print unreachable destinations when no path exists.) Tj T*
(- To produce deterministic outputs by sorting adjacency lists.) Tj T*
(Introduction) Tj T*
(Graphs are one of the most important data structures used in computer science. Many) Tj T*
(real-world problems such as road navigation, computer networks, social networks,) Tj T*
(transportation systems, and campus maps can be represented using graphs.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(In this project, every building or location inside the campus is represented as a vertex,) Tj T*
(while every walkway connecting two buildings is represented as an edge.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Example:) Tj T*
(Academic Block ----- Library) Tj T*
(        |) Tj T*
(        |) Tj T*
(Computer Lab ----- Cafeteria) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Hostel ----- Playground) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(This graph contains two connected areas.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The first connected area contains:) Tj T*
(Academic Block) Tj T*
(Library) Tj T*
(Computer Lab) Tj T*
(Cafeteria) Tj T*
() Tj T*
ET
